/* life — Conway's Game of Life with a real screen.

   `examples/algorithms/gameoflife.cufe` is the matrix edition, and it is about
   the board. This one is about the TERMINAL: it clears the screen, draws in
   place, and waits for you between generations.

       cufet examples/systems/life.cufe        ← press Enter to step, q to quit

   ── Why this program exists ──

   ★★ It could not be written in Cufet at all until the day it was. Drawing in
   place needs an ESC character, and a text literal escapes exactly seven things
   — none of them ESC — so an escape sequence had no spelling anywhere in the
   language. Clearing a terminal, the most ordinary thing a full-screen program
   does, required reaching into C.

   `Insert 27 into <chase>` is what fixed that: a chase holds code points, so a
   number names a character directly. This program is the reason that exists,
   which is the "no ordinary capability should require an axiom" campaign
   working the way it should — a real program finding the gap, rather than the
   gap being guessed at.

   ── Two ways to run, and why ──

   ⚠ With a terminal it is interactive. With no input at all — which is how the
   oracle runs every example — `read a line from the input` is void straight
   away, and the program prints a fixed number of generations plainly instead,
   with no escape sequences at all.

   ★ That is not a test accommodation. It is what a well-behaved program does
   when nobody is there: a tool that emits cursor-control codes into a pipe is
   a tool that has not checked. The corpus's other script does the same thing —
   `search.cufe` searches the file that ships beside it when given no arguments,
   "so that reading the example and running the example tell the same story".

   ⚠ The interactive half is therefore NOT covered by the suite. Only the
   fallback is. That is worth knowing rather than assuming.
*/

Pull a book on collections.
    /* The escape sequences, built the only way they can be: a chase, because a
       number in one is a code point. 27 is ESC; "[2J" clears; "[H" homes. */
    Bind text to screen-clear:
        Define out as a chase.
        Insert 27 into out.
        Insert "[2J" into out.
        Insert 27 into out.
        Insert "[H" into out.
        Return out converted to text.
    Done.

    /* Edges wrap, so a glider travels forever instead of walking off. */
    Bind number to wrapped, given (the number spot, the number extent):
        If spot is less than 1, return spot + extent.
        If spot is greater than extent, return spot - extent.
        Return spot.
    Done.

    Bind fact to live-at, given (the series of text board, the number down, the number across):
        Define height as the number of board.
        Define width as the length of item 1 of board.
        Define row as item (cast wrapped on (down, height)) of board.
        Define spot as cast wrapped on (across, width).
        Return the characters from spot to spot of row is "#".
    Done.

    Bind number to neighbours, given (the series of text board, the number down, the number across):
        Define total as 0.
        For each step-down in range 0 - 1 to 1, repeat:
            For each step-across in range 0 - 1 to 1, repeat:
                If step-down is 0:
                    If step-across is 0, skip.
                Done.
                If cast live-at on (board, down + step-down, across + step-across):
                    Increment total by 1.
                Done.
            Done.
        Done.
        Return total.
    Done.

    /* One generation. ★ A chase per row rather than joining text: a text is
       immutable, so building a row a character at a time by joining would
       rebuild the whole row every time — which is the quadratic this type
       exists to avoid. */
    Bind series of text to stepped, given (the series of text board):
        Define height as the number of board.
        Define width as the length of item 1 of board.
        Define next-board as a series of text.

        For each down in range 1 to height, repeat:
            Define row as a chase.
            For each across in range 1 to width, repeat:
                Define count as cast neighbours on (board, down, across).
                Define here as cast live-at on (board, down, across).

                Define lives as false.
                If here:
                    If count is 2, the lives becomes true.
                    If count is 3, the lives becomes true.
                Done.
                Otherwise:
                    If count is 3, the lives becomes true.
                Done.

                If lives, insert "#" into row.
                Otherwise, insert "." into row.
            Done.
            Insert row converted to text into next-board.
        Done.
        Return next-board.
    Done.

    Bind void to draw, given (the series of text board, the number generation, the fact in-place):
        If in-place, state cast screen-clear.
        State "life — generation {generation converted to text}".
        State "".
        For each row in board, repeat:
            State row.
        Done.
    Done.

    Bind void to play:
        /* A glider on a 12 by 8 board. It travels down and right, and wraps. */
        Define board as a series of text with (
            ".#..........",
            "..#.........",
            "###.........",
            "............",
            "............",
            "............",
            "............",
            "............").

        /* ⚠ ONE read decides which program this is. `read a line from the input`
           is void at end of input, and with nobody there it is void immediately —
           so this asks "is anyone out there" and answers it once. */
        Define first as read a line from the input.
        Define interactive as first is not void.

        If interactive is false:
            /* Nobody there: four generations, plainly, no escape sequences. */
            Define generation as 0.
            Cast draw on (board, generation, false).
            For each tick in range 1 to 4, repeat:
                The board becomes cast stepped on (board).
                Increment generation by 1.
                State "".
                Cast draw on (board, generation, false).
            Done.
            Exit.
        Done.

        /* Somebody is there. The line already read is the first Enter. */
        Define generation as 0.
        Cast draw on (board, generation, true).
        State "".
        State "Enter to step, q to quit.".

        Define going as true.
        While going, repeat:
            Define typed as read a line from the input.
            If typed is void, the going becomes false.
            Otherwise:
                If typed is "q", the going becomes false.
                Otherwise:
                    The board becomes cast stepped on (board).
                    Increment generation by 1.
                    Cast draw on (board, generation, true).
                    State "".
                    State "Enter to step, q to quit.".
                Done.
            Done.
        Done.
        Exit.
    Done.

    Cast play.
Done.
